 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+### §check-fails-closed — a failing command inside the check aborts it with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. Large staged files are
+  still only warned about; the script ends in `exit 0` when nothing inside it failed. The loop uses `if`, not
+  `[ ... ] && echo`, so a false test is not mistaken for a failure.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e` to make internal errors silent again.
+- Reason: the maintainer decided the check was too forgiving: internal breakage went unnoticed. The maintainer
+  overrode §check-fails-open (below) on 2026-10-23.
+- Risk accepted: the incident behind the old decision (a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file blocked all
+  commits for a day, 2026-09-10) is guarded by the `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` check but could recur with other failures.
+
+### §check-fails-open — SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §check-fails-closed
 - Since: 2026-09-11
 - Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
 - Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
